  "identity": {
    "playing_style": "Under Rui Borges, Sporting build patiently with one midfielder (formerly Hjulmand) dropping between the centre-backs and the right-back tucking inside, then attack at volume - 89 league goals (best in Portugal) in 2025/26 and 2.2 goals per game across his first 89 matches. Borges has never coached a back-three side by preference and has gradually moved from Amorim's 3-4-3 to his own back-four model, prioritising more defensive balance than his predecessor. The 2026/27 side is a rebuilt, very young group (average age around 21) after record sales.",
    "principles": [
      "develop-and-sell at record prices (Quenda EUR 50m, Trincao ~EUR 44m, Hjulmand EUR 40m+5m, Diomande EUR 40m) with huge release clauses on new signings (EUR 80m on Altimira, Ba, Kaique)",
      "reinvest sales into young, high-upside profiles (10 signings for 2026/27, EUR 116m+ spent)",
      "coach continuity: Rui Borges contracted to 2028",
      "academy pathway (Alcochete) - Rafael Nel handed the no. 9, Flavio Goncalves and Joao Simoes in the senior group",
      "win the title while competing in the UCL after two league titles (2024, 2025) and a runner-up finish"
    ],
    "club_character": "One of Portugal's 'big three', with a large, passionate fanbase at the Estadio Jose Alvalade and heavy daily coverage from A Bola, Record and O Jogo. High pressure after losing the title to Porto and the Taca de Portugal final to second-tier Torreense in May 2026; fans are watching a wholesale squad rebuild after the biggest sales window in club history.",
    "ownership": "Member-owned club (Sporting Clube de Portugal) controlling Sporting SAD (listed). President Frederico Varandas re-elected 14 March 2026 with 89.47% of votes for a third mandate to 2030; he also chairs the SAD board (Salgado Zenha, Bernardo, Dias Ferreira, Pinto de Abreu as members).",
    "political_and_social_context": "Publicly documented: the SAD approved a bonus for Varandas and the board after the title-winning seasons (Soccerway/press); summer 2026 saw an unprecedented sales programme (EUR 117m+ received by early August, A Bola) that was publicly debated in the Portuguese press. No institution-level controversies beyond that were found in this research.",
    "languages": [
      "pt",
      "es",
      "en",
      "fr"
    ],
    "wage_structure": "unknown (Luis Suarez and the senior internationals presumed at the top - not verified)",
    "transfer_policy": "Sell at peak to the Premier League, Saudi Arabia and Spain; buy 20-24-year-olds from Portugal (Zalazar EUR 30m from Braga, Ba EUR 21m from Famalicao, Lima EUR 4m from AVS), Serie A/La Liga (Doumbia, Altimira) and smaller leagues (Andersen from Hacken, Zekri from Mechelen, Kaique from Palmeiras), plus one Premier League loan (Derry). Standard EUR 80m clauses.",
    "injury_management_reputation": "Nuno Santos and St. Juste were handed to the medical department at the July 2026 presentation; no wider public criticism of the medical department found."
